WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.380
Let us understand mutually exclusive events

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.780
with another example.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.120
Let us consider one throw of a fair dice.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.880
These are the six possible outcomes.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.860
At one throw, you have to get one of these.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.720
Let us consider two events, 'A' and 'B'

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:24.900
and understand if they are mutually exclusive or not.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.120
Event 'A' is getting an even number.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.700
And event 'B' is getting an odd number.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.420
Remember, we have to consider just one throw.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.480
Assume we get a two at the first throw.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:44.200
It is even and not odd at the second throw we get a 5.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.180
So it is odd and not even.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.880
This means that if we get an odd number,

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:51.960
we know that it is not even.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.940
And if we get an even number it cannot be odd.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:59.380
These two events cannot occur together

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.460
and are hence mutually exclusive events.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:04.940
Now let's look at another case.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.380
Say event 'A' is getting an odd number.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:12.020
And event 'B' is getting a number less than four.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.020
Are these events mutually exclusive?

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.500
Let's see!

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.980
If we get a 1 then we can see

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:22.680
that it's an odd number as well as a number less than 4.

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.280
It's the same if we get a 3.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.860
These two events can happen together

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.820
which tells us that they are not mutually exclusive.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.220
Many get confused between Independent and mutually exclusive events.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:42.640
If you are one among those, just remember these two simple things.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.140
Two events are independent if they are not related.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.440
That is the outcome of one does not affect the other.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.840
They are totally unrelated.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.680
And two events are mutually exclusive

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.040
if they cannot occur together.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:02.840
If one event occurs, the other just cannot occur.

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:06.360
So yes, an event cannot be independent

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:09.260
and mutually exclusive at the same time.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.920
Because if they're independent they may occur together.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:15.260
And if they are mutually exclusive

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:18.500
one event will be dependent on the other.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.740
That is, if one occurs, the other one will not occur.

